I ran all 9 steps of `収集手順.md`. This was the Thursday 11/12 0:00 run, and it added **113 records** to `data/catch-log.js`.

**Missed runs:** the 11/11 12:00 and 18:00 runs never happened (the data file and run log both stopped at 6:00), so this run caught up on everything posted since then. The cause wasn't investigated; the PC may have been asleep.

**Steps 1–4 (collection):**
- **chowari:** I read pages 1–80 and found 135 new reports in the target areas, which became 112 records:
  - by catch date: 11/11 70, 11/10 27, 11/09 5, 11/08 10
  - by area: 東京湾 56 (湾奥 28, 湾口三浦 21, 房総 7), 相模湾 45, 駿河湾 11
- **かめや釣具:** 1 record (沼津サーフ タチウオ 5匹, 11/10). Their 相模川河口 シーバス report has the same date, spot and species as an existing record, so the duplicate rule skipped it.
- **Other shop blogs:** nothing new in the target areas. Everything new at イシグロ was outside the areas or the fish list.
- **YouTube:** one search found no usable videos.
- **Not recorded:** reports with no target species (tuna-only, フグ, ハゼ, マダコ, ヤリイカ and other squid) and reports outside the target areas.

**Steps 5–6 (data file):** the new records are at the top of `window.CATCH_LOG`. `lastUpdated` is "2026-11-12 00:00", and the note gives the counts above and says why this run was a catch-up.

**Step 7:** I wrote `logs/2026-11-12.md` with the URLs checked, what was rejected and why, and judgment calls such as name changes (シロアマダイ→アマダイ, ヒラソウダ→ソウダガツオ) and merged posts.

**Step 8:** the file loads in node with 3908 records (3795 + 113). There are no duplicate ids or duplicate date+spot+species entries, every record has a source URL, and all new fish are on the target list.

**Step 9:** I stopped there with no commit and no HTML rebuild, which `run-collect.ps1` handles. The only changes in `釣り/` are `data/catch-log.js` and `logs/2026-11-12.md`. Temporary files and a backup of `catch-log.js` from before the edit are in `%TEMP%\t1112a`.

